    approach: [{"title": "Initial Assessment", "body": "We begin by listening. An unhurried review of your symptoms, history, current and past medications, medical conditions, stressors and goals gives us the full picture before any decision is made.", "iconPath": "M9 12h6m-6 4h6m2 5H7a2 2 0 01-2-2V5a2 2 0 012-2h5.586a1 1 0 01.707.293l5.414 5.414a1 1 0 01.293.707V19a2 2 0 01-2 2z"}, {"title": "Personalized Plan", "body": "No two people experience mental health conditions the same way. Together we discuss options, expected benefits and possible side effects, and build a plan around your diagnosis, preferences and daily life.", "iconPath": "M4 6h16M4 12h16M4 18h7"}, {"title": "Ongoing Support", "body": "Follow-up visits track how you are responding. Doses can be adjusted, medications changed or plans updated as your needs evolve. Care works best as a steady partnership rather than a one-time event.", "iconPath": "M4.318 6.318a4.5 4.5 0 000 6.364L12 20.364l7.682-7.682a4.5 4.5 0 00-6.364-6.364L12 7.636l-1.318-1.318a4.5 4.5 0 00-6.364 0z"}],
    benefits: [{"title": "Individualized Plans", "body": "Sometimes it is medication, sometimes therapy, often a combination of both. The plan is built around your needs rather than a one-size-fits-all approach."}, {"title": "Careful Monitoring", "body": "Regular follow-up helps catch side effects early and keeps treatment matched to how you are actually doing."}, {"title": "Combined Approaches", "body": "Studies show that combining approaches often helps people who do not improve with a single one."}, {"title": "Local, Consistent Follow-Up", "body": "For people in Tooele and nearby communities, local care, in the office or by virtual visit, makes follow-up easier to keep."}],
    timelineHeading: "What to Expect",
    timeline: [{"title": "First Visit", "body": "A detailed conversation about your symptoms, history and goals. If medication is appropriate, we talk through the options and decide on next steps together."}, {"title": "Week 2-4", "body": "A check-in on how you are tolerating any medication and how therapy strategies are fitting into daily life. Many medications need several weeks to show their full effect."}, {"title": "Month 1-3", "body": "Fine-tuning. Doses or approaches may be adjusted, and we review benefits, side effects and whether the plan fits your daily life."}, {"title": "Long-term", "body": "Care shifts toward maintaining progress and preventing relapse. We periodically reassess whether your medication is still the right choice for you."}],
    extraSections: [{"heading": "Medications and therapies", "body": ["Depending on your diagnosis, treatment may involve antidepressants, anti-anxiety medications, mood stabilizers, antipsychotics, or medications for attention and sleep. Each class works differently, and we explain what to expect, including common side effects and what to report.", "Medication often works best alongside other supports. Care may include evidence-based talk therapy such as cognitive behavioral therapy or brief solution-focused therapy, along with sleep habits, physical activity, stress management and coordination with other health professionals so your care fits together."]}, {"heading": "A Note on Recovery", "body": ["Recovery is rarely a straight line. Good days and harder days are normal, and your plan can change as you do. Every person responds differently, and timelines are general guides rather than promises."]}],
    faqs: [{"q": "What conditions can treatment help with?", "a": "Mental health treatment is commonly used for depression, anxiety, ADHD, bipolar disorder, trauma-related conditions, obsessive-compulsive disorder and other concerns that affect mood, thinking and behavior. An assessment helps determine what applies to you."}, {"q": "Will I need medication?", "a": "Not necessarily. Some people benefit from medication, some from therapy, and many from a combination. The decision is made together, based on your symptoms, preferences and medical history."}, {"q": "How soon will I feel a difference?", "a": "Timing depends on the treatment. Some effects, like improved sleep, may appear sooner, while others such as antidepressant benefits commonly take several weeks. Patience and follow-up are important."}, {"q": "How long will I need to take medication?", "a": "It varies by person and condition. Some people take medication for a limited period, while others benefit from longer-term treatment. This is a decision we revisit together over time."}, {"q": "Can I stop my medication if I feel better?", "a": "Please talk with your prescriber first. Stopping suddenly can cause withdrawal effects or a return of symptoms, and a gradual, supervised plan is usually safer."}, {"q": "Do you prescribe controlled substances?", "a": "We can, and often will, prescribe controlled substances, but only on an individual basis and only if we agree that the benefit of the medication outweighs the risks. Controlled substances are not prescribed at your first visit, and you will need to be seen in office periodically if you receive them from us. We might ask for outside records or drug screens to ensure your safety and well-being. If, after a careful assessment, we do not agree with a long-standing medication, we can explore other options and, if necessary, create a taper plan."}, {"q": "How do I get started at Blue Willow Psychiatry?", "a": "Call (435) 200-3430 or use our contact page. We are located in Mindful Collective at 1102 W 60 S, Ste 6, Tooele, Utah 84074, and we can answer questions about next steps."}],
